  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Sunday)\nIssued at 118 PM MDT Sun Jul 19 2026\n\nUpper air analysis continues to show a strong ridge sitting across \nmuch of the western CONUS, centered across CO/UT. Several waves have \nweakened and broadened out the northern edge of the ridge, resulting \nin more zonal flow over the the northern Plains. Surface obs show a \nsurface low over Sask with cooler air pushing southward into NW \nMT/NE ND. Ahead of it...very hot temps continue across SE MT through \nthe CWA but with an exception. A weak surface trough is extending \nacross central portions of western SD into NE WY. KUDX radar shows \nlight showers extending along the Cheyenne River valley to Rapid \nCity to Newcastle. Just trace amounts are expected with these \nshowers, but they are keeping temps cool, in the 80s. However, the \ntemperature relief will be short-lived. Temps in the mid to upper \n90s across NW SD are following the band of showers/clouds, which \nwill increase temps over the Rapid City area later this afternoon. \nThere is still uncertainty as to how the storm development will \nevolve later this afternoon. Instability will be moderate this \nafternoon. Linear shear will be best across NW SD, but overall the \ningredients for organized severe wx will be better east of the CWA. \nWith the dry boundary, wind will be the main threat with any storms \nthat form, though some storms could briefly have hail. Convection \nwill exit to the east this evening. Smoke from wildfires primarily \nacross the Pacific NW is expected to move in from the north \novernight and remain through Monday before perhaps exiting Monday\nnight or Tuesday. Can't rule out early morning showers across the\nCWA Monday morning as the main front pushes through. Highs will \nagain be warm on Monday, but think the near-100 degree heat should\nbe limited to south of I-90. There is still some question as to \nwhether a heat advisory extension may be needed for some of the \nwestern SD plains, but will let the evening shift reassess as the \ncurrent advisory approaches expiration. Generally dry weather is \nexpected through Tuesday, with much cooler temperatures in the \n80s. Upper flow becomes more northwesterly, but models are hinting\nat a wave crossing the region through Tuesday night, which could \nbring another round of showers and thunderstorms thorugh Wednesday\nmorning. As the ridge axis finally moves eastward toward the High\nPlains, an increase in monsoonal moisture is expected. GFS \nestimates PWATs of 150-175% of normal. With a series of strong, \nslow-moving waves crossing the region in the Wed/Thu \ntimeframe...tapping into rich moisture, there is a possibility \nsome portions of the northern Plains could see much more ample \namounts of badly-needed rainfall. Temps will stay in the \n80s...then models are suggesting a reestablishment of the ridge \ntoward the end of the week, which could bring back hot \ntemperatures.",
